Parent: The Ray-sweeping algorithm is more efficient than the Warm-up Algorithm because it leverages an observation that allows for constant time updates of skew values while sweeping over A\ud835\udc5b\n2, which makes its running time equal to the enumeration of the \ud835\udc5b\n2-th level of arrangement. This results in a significant improvement from the Warm-up algorithm's super-quadratic complexity.
[all_together_subsections] parent=Minoria Mining in 2D sample=2 attempt=4/5
The Parent-child relationship between the two algorithms is that they both aim to identify high skew directions, but they differ in their approach and efficiency. The Warm-up algorithm enumerates all possible projections of a given parent node by computing the medians for each vertex in A\ud835b\n2 and then identifying the top 10% highest skew values to identify high-skew projections. However, the Ray-sweeping algorithm is more efficient as it leverages our earlier observation to compute medians more efficiently by sweeping a ray counterclockwise from the x-axis to the y-axis and maintaining some aggregates while sweeping over A\ud835b\n2, which enables updating the skew values in constant time. 

The Ray-sweeping algorithm is similar to the Warm-up Algorithm but with an improved efficiency. It follows the three steps of (1) enumerating A\ud835b\n2, followed by (2) computing the medians and (3) identifying the high-skew projections. 

The time complexity of both algorithms is dominated by step two: calculating each skew value in a constant time (except for the very first vertex). The running time of the second part is equal to the time taken for enumerating the \ud835b\n2-th level of the arrangement  – i.e., 

The Parent node's children are:
1: Warm-up Algorithm, which has a super-quadratic running time and is not efficient.
2: Ray-sweeping algorithm that maintains some aggregates while sweeping over A\ud835b\n2, which enables updating the skew values in constant time. 

The pseudo-code of this approach is provided in Algorithm 1. A visualization of this algorithm on a toy example is provided in Figure 3.
